WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.600
WECHAT:
If you follow the tech world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:07.520
you’re probably familiar with the Chinese giant&nbsp;
Tencent. They are the largest Chinese tech company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:14.120
with a market cap of $376 billion and they have&nbsp;
their arms in everything from gaming and social&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:20.480
media to payments and investing. But likely their&nbsp;
most notable product is their super app WeChat.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:28.400
WeChat is basically ubiquitous with a total of 827&nbsp;
million users within just China. And this ubiquity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:33.480
isn’t surprising given that WeChat attempts to&nbsp;
do everything. You can buy groceries, book planes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:38.120
and hotels, make payments, message people, read&nbsp;
the news, get food delivery, have a video call,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:43.080
play video games, and basically whatever else you&nbsp;
want. For years, American companies have looked at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:50.320
WeChat with pure envy as they too want a super app&nbsp;
that encompasses every aspect of people’s lives.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:55.800
And it seems that the latest tech billionaire to&nbsp;
jump on this band wagon is Elon Musk who wants to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.800 --> 00:01:01.760
turn X into an everything app. He says that for&nbsp;
X to succeed, the platform needs to compete with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:07.720
YouTube, LinkedIn, Facetime, dating apps, and for&nbsp;
some reason, the entire banking industry. Given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:12.480
that Elon has basically infinite resources,&nbsp;
actually developing such an app and putting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:18.440
in front of people would not be too difficult,&nbsp;
but actually getting people to use it, well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:23.200
that’s a whole nother story. Google, Facebook,&nbsp;
and Apple have been trying to do this for over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:29.320
a decade now, slowly expanding to one industry&nbsp;
after another. But to this day, they’re all very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:34.840
much dependent on their hero product. 58% of all&nbsp;
of Google’s revenue for example still comes from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:41.000
search ads. 52% of Apple’s revenue still comes&nbsp;
from the iPhone. And 96% of Meta’s revenue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:46.400
still comes from just Facebook and Instagram.&nbsp;
And that’s despite these companies expanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:52.720
to dozens and dozens of sectors and industries&nbsp;
often betting as much as a $100 billion. Clearly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:58.440
infinite money and resources haven’t allowed any&nbsp;
of these guys to create a true super app. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:10.063
here’s why a super app has never worked in&nbsp;
America and why X won't be any different.

00:02:10.063 --> 00:02:12.760
PUBLIC HESITANCE:
Likely the biggest factor preventing a super app&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:18.400
from blowing up in America is public hesitance.&nbsp;
The majority of the public already feels that big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:23.400
tech has too much power and they feel no need to&nbsp;
start using even more offerings from any one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:28.680
these companies. And whenever a big tech company&nbsp;
tries to push such a vision, people not only tend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:35.400
to not buy in but often resist. Likely the best&nbsp;
example of this was with Google+. If you’re not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:40.000
familiar with Google+, it was basically Google’s&nbsp;
attempt to take on Facebook and create their own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:43.240
social media platform. They were relatively&nbsp;
early to social media as well given that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:49.720
network launched in mid 2011. On paper, Google+&nbsp;
actually started off pretty strong. Within 2 weeks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:55.200
of launch, they got 10 million users. Within a&nbsp;
month, they had 25 million users. And within 2&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.200 --> 00:03:02.680
years, they had over half a billion users. Surely,&nbsp;
this was a massive success right? Well, that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:08.640
until you peel back the layers. You see, while&nbsp;
Google was able to get a bunch of users on board,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:12.040
these weren’t exactly excited or loyal users.&nbsp;
Rather, Google+ was forced onto these users. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:16.840
example, Google made it nearly impossible to&nbsp;
open a Gmail account without creating a Google+&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:21.600
profile. They also locked a bunch of useful&nbsp;
functionality behind a Google+. If you wanted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:26.200
see reviews from your friends, you needed Google+.&nbsp;
If you wanted to see the leaderboards on the Play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:32.280
Store, you needed Google+. If you wanted to use&nbsp;
Google Hangouts, you needed Google+. Likely the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:37.240
worst infraction though was when Google required&nbsp;
users to have a Google+ account to leave comments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:42.760
on YouTube. This move generated so much backlash&nbsp;
that one of YouTube’s founders, Jawed Karim,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:47.840
came out of his 8 year slumber to say “why the&nbsp;
f do i need a google+ account to comment on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:52.560
video?” All of this backlash would lead to&nbsp;
Gooogle rolling back many of these policies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:57.200
but it’s not like things would’ve played out&nbsp;
any differently even if they didn’t roll these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:02.520
back. The reality was that while they were&nbsp;
able to get over half a billion accounts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:08.400
90% of user sessions lasted less than 5 seconds,&nbsp;
and Google would eventually end up shutting down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:14.240
the platform in 2019. Now, you might be inclined&nbsp;
to say that this failure was largely just because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:20.000
Google didn’t understand the social media space,&nbsp;
but honestly I don’t think that would’ve made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:25.680
much of a difference which brings us into our&nbsp;
next example: Threads. I think we can all agree&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:31.040
that Meta is the undisputed king of social media&nbsp;
with multiple platforms with billions of users&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:35.120
each. So, you would think that they would&nbsp;
have a great shot at taking down Twitter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:39.840
especially given the poor optics after&nbsp;
Elon’s takeover. And to Threads credit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:45.760
they did have an insane launch reaching 100&nbsp;
million users within just 5 days. But, this was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:51.480
followed by an explosive fall as well as Threads&nbsp;
would lose 80% of its daily active users within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:57.480
just 1 month. And Google+ and Threads are just&nbsp;
2 notable examples. There’s plenty of examples&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.480 --> 00:05:03.320
of this across tech such as the Microsoft Zune,&nbsp;
the Windows Phone, Bing, Edge, the Google Pixel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.320 --> 00:05:08.040
Google Meets, Google Classroom, Bing Maps,&nbsp;
Google Bard, and on and on and on. It seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:12.800
that these tech leaders think that people will be&nbsp;
super ecstatic about having yet another big tech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:18.760
account or service. They expect people to be like&nbsp;
“OH WOW! I can do Twitter on Threads now??? This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.760 --> 00:05:24.240
is a gamechanger. I can’t wait to ditch Twitter.”&nbsp;
In reality, people are more like “Oh cool,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:29.080
Meta has a new app now? I’ll check it out.” None&nbsp;
of this is to say that these companies can’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.080 --> 00:05:34.600
launch a successful product in an adjacent or even&nbsp;
completely different industry. But the bottomline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:39.920
is that these company’s resources and ubiquity&nbsp;
doesn’t exactly make it any more likely that these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:45.640
new products succeed especially on the scale that&nbsp;
you would expect from these companies because it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.640 --> 00:05:56.062
only these companies and their leaders who are&nbsp;
jumping up and down about the idea of a super app.

00:05:56.062 --> 00:05:57.760
LACK OF KNOWLEDGE:
Sometimes, big tech is able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.760 --> 00:06:02.240
overcome the odds and launch a new successful&nbsp;
widely used product in a completely different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:07.920
industry. But they’re not home free yet because&nbsp;
now, they have to make the venture actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:14.040
worthwhile which is an even bigger challenge.&nbsp;
Likely the best example of this is Apple’s foray&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:19.680
into the financial industry. In early 2019, Apple&nbsp;
announced that they were gonna launch a credit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:25.000
card. A credit card that easily integrated into&nbsp;
the Apple ecosystem, came with a beautiful UI,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:30.200
and encouraged strong financial habits. From the&nbsp;
very beginning, credit card enthusiasts weren’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.200 --> 00:06:35.280
a big fan of this card. While they didn’t have&nbsp;
anything particularly bad to say about the card,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:39.960
they simply felt that there were better options&nbsp;
on the market in terms of rewards and benefits.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:45.280
But despite their concerns, the Apple card would&nbsp;
go onto be a massive success amongst the general&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:51.520
public growing to over 6 million card holders. It&nbsp;
turns out that the average person far preferred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.520 --> 00:06:56.160
the simplicity and convenience of the Apple Card&nbsp;
over maximizing credit card points with Amex&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.160 --> 00:07:03.080
or Chase. And that’s still the case to this day,&nbsp;
people still really like the Apple Card. The same,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.080 --> 00:07:07.560
however, cannot be said about Goldman Sachs,&nbsp;
the issuer of the Apple Card who has lost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:12.880
over $1 billion largely due to Apple’s lack of&nbsp;
understanding of the credit card market. You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:18.200
Apple largely approached the Apple Card with the&nbsp;
tech mindset and that’s largely why it was so well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:24.200
received but this was also the card’s biggest&nbsp;
pitfall. For example, with tech, you want your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.200 --> 00:07:29.200
products to be as widely accessible as possible.&nbsp;
That’s why Apple kept the credit requirements&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.200 --> 00:07:34.480
for the Apple Card super low. They wanted it to&nbsp;
be as easily accessible as possible and make it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.480 --> 00:07:39.760
easy for first time credit card holders to build&nbsp;
their credit. But, while that’s a great sentiment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.760 --> 00:07:43.720
it also led to a quarter of their credit card&nbsp;
loans going out to people with credit scores&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.720 --> 00:07:51.200
below 660 leading to the worst bank loss rate of&nbsp;
any big US card issuer. But not only is the Apple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.200 --> 00:07:56.200
Card losing money on its sub prime customers but&nbsp;
they’re also not making any money on their good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.200 --> 00:08:01.000
standing customers. Thanks to the Apple’s Cards&nbsp;
great UI and insistence that you pay off your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:06.200
credit card bills, making money on good standing&nbsp;
customers is also nearly impossible. This isn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.200 --> 00:08:11.560
to say that Apple has to be predatory in order to&nbsp;
make money. Many Amex cards, for example, don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.560 --> 00:08:16.960
even allow customers to carry balances, but Amex&nbsp;
is still able to make a bunch of money from these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:22.160
customers because they charge annual membership&nbsp;
fees and have other modes of monetization. Apple,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.160 --> 00:08:27.480
however, is stuck in a very tricky situation.&nbsp;
Their sub prime customers are defaulting left and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.480 --> 00:08:31.840
right, credit card enthusiasts don’t want anything&nbsp;
to do with the Apple Card, and their average&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:37.240
customers always pay off their entire balance and&nbsp;
never pay any interest. Yeah, I don’t think you’d&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.240 --> 00:08:41.520
be surprised to hear that Apple has agreed to end&nbsp;
their credit card and savings account partnerships&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:47.240
with Goldman Sachs. But honestly, I don’t think&nbsp;
Apple could’ve really avoided this fate. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.240 --> 00:08:52.800
reality is that it takes time to truly understand&nbsp;
and compete in an industry such as credit cards.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.800 --> 00:08:57.720
Immediately launching to 6 million card holders&nbsp;
much of whom have never had a credit card before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.720 --> 00:09:08.946
is bound to cause issues which brings me into the&nbsp;
fundamental difference between China and America.

00:09:08.946 --> 00:09:11.240
A FREE MARKET:
I don’t want to get too political here but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.240 --> 00:09:16.640
reality is that launching a product in America and&nbsp;
China are very different. For a product to truly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.640 --> 00:09:22.000
be successful in China, you very much need the&nbsp;
blessing of the CCP which companies like Tencent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:26.840
already have. And that’s why they’re able to&nbsp;
expand to any industry or sector with ease.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.840 --> 00:09:32.320
They’re often the biggest player in that industry&nbsp;
or they’re even the first player in that industry.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:37.640
This makes capturing market share a walk in the&nbsp;
park but it’s completely different in America.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.640 --> 00:09:42.120
When an American company tries to create a super&nbsp;
app and expand to different industries, they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.120 --> 00:09:46.840
competing with highly established and competitive&nbsp;
players and brands. Apple for example was taking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.840 --> 00:09:52.840
on American Express, Chase, Citi, Capital one, and&nbsp;
all the other big banks. But, we don’t even have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.840 --> 00:09:57.840
to look into a different industry. This is true&nbsp;
within the tech industry itself. With Google+,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.840 --> 00:10:03.160
Google was competing against Meta. With Bing and&nbsp;
Edge, Microsoft was competing against Google.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.160 --> 00:10:10.200
With the Windows Phone, Microsoft was competing&nbsp;
against Apple and Google, and so on. Any industry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.200 --> 00:10:15.160
that these guys want to expand to is already&nbsp;
dominated by one of their biggest rivals and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.160 --> 00:10:20.760
unless these guys let up or drop the ball for some&nbsp;
reason, expanding here is nearly impossible. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.760 --> 00:10:26.280
ironically, one of the biggest factors preventing&nbsp;
a Tencent style monopoly is the big tech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.280 --> 00:10:32.280
oligopaly. None of them are going to let the other&nbsp;
eat their lunch. But even putting that aside,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.280 --> 00:10:38.200
Americans simply aren’t interested in super app.&nbsp;
In fact, they actually like having separation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.200 --> 00:10:42.960
Take the financial industry for example. You can&nbsp;
have basically any one of your financial needs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.960 --> 00:10:48.880
be taken care of by Chase or Wells Fargo or any&nbsp;
one of the big banks. But the average American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.880 --> 00:10:56.560
doesn’t just have 1 Chase account, they actually&nbsp;
have 25-40 financial accounts. They have a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.560 --> 00:11:01.520
checking accounts, a few savings accounts, 1 or 2&nbsp;
HSA accounts, a couple of credits cards, a couple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.520 --> 00:11:06.680
of payment platforms, a couple of investing and&nbsp;
trading platforms, a few loan providers, and a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.680 --> 00:11:12.800
financial managment software providers. The reason&nbsp;
being that one they like separation, and two, each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.800 --> 00:11:17.600
account is good a something specfiic whether that&nbsp;
be investing or credit cards or mobile payments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.600 --> 00:11:23.280
or whatever. It’s the same thing with tech.&nbsp;
People have their favorite social media apps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.280 --> 00:11:27.360
their messaging apps, their streaming services,&nbsp;
their games, their browsers, their emails, their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.360 --> 00:11:32.480
operating systems, and so on. Americans tend to&nbsp;
choose the one the like the most from each sector&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.480 --> 00:11:37.360
to create an assortment of tech services that&nbsp;
let them enjoy the best of all words like a true&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.360 --> 00:11:43.520
free market. And until that changes and Americans&nbsp;
want a product just because its from X or Meta or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.520 --> 00:11:50.040
Google or whatever, big tech really has no chance&nbsp;
of creating a true super app. With historically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.040 --> 00:11:55.400
high interest rates, the appeal bills and bonds is&nbsp;
the higher than ever, yet traditional brokerages&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.400 --> 00:12:00.480
haven’t really adapted with the times. They&nbsp;
still expect people to use clunky bond tables,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.480 --> 00:12:06.680
scroll through long overwhleming lists, and even&nbsp;
